<p><strong>A studio stops overbooking when every class has a capacity limit, bookings close at that number and extra students join a waiting list.</strong></p>
<p><em>The scene below is a typical one, not a single named customer.</em></p>
<h2>Fifteen students, twelve mats</h2>
<p>A popular 7 am class is booked by message, by phone and at the desk. Nobody is counting. Fifteen students arrive for twelve mats, someone is turned away at the door, and the instructor starts the class already on the back foot.</p>
<h2>Why classes get overbooked</h2>
<ul><li>No fixed limit written next to the class.</li><li>Bookings from several places.</li><li>No clear way to say "full" or offer a waiting list.</li></ul>
<h2>How a studio can manage capacity</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Set a capacity</strong> for each class from the room, equipment and safety.</li><li><strong>Count every booking in one place</strong> so the number is real.</li><li><strong>Close the class when full</strong> and offer the next one.</li><li><strong>Keep a waiting list</strong> and offer cancelled spots quickly. See <a href="/blog/waiting-list-management-spa-salon-nepal/">waiting list management</a>.</li></ol>
